Drives its own private GC state (`state = []i64(gcWords)`, bound via
`gcBindMemory`/`gcInit` to its own arena) and its own collection driver
(`chanDriveCollection`, called explicitly twice), not the ambient runtime
collector — the two `print`ed scans assert on THAT collector's own counts
(`gcNumObjects`/`gcStatTotalSwept`), which are correct regardless of whether
the ambient (libbitrt) collector ever runs at all. Every allocation here goes
through this private `g` handle, never the ambient one, so the ambient
collector has nothing live to find and legitimately never fires under
`BIT_GC=stress`. The backing arenas are held live in `main` for the whole
program, which is what keeps the raw pointers into them valid should the
ambient collector run anyway. Same shape as tests/stress/chanmakeroots, which
builds the channel through `chan_make` instead of by hand.
